\begin{abstract}
We present the Sovereign Regenerative Engine (SRE), a multi-scale simulation architecture
for controlled tissue reconstruction under adversarial biological conditions. The engine
integrates cellular proliferation control, scaffold-level mechanical stability, and systemic
metabolic and immune balance into a unified invariant framework. We formalize the governing
equations, define a global regeneration invariant, and demonstrate Lyapunov-stable convergence
under extreme stress-testing vectors, including hyper-proliferative shock, scaffold degradation,
and metabolic/immune depletion.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Tissue regeneration involves tightly coupled processes across cellular, structural, and systemic
scales. Uncontrolled proliferation, scaffold failure, or metabolic collapse can lead to
pathological outcomes such as neoplastic drift or non-functional tissue.

The Sovereign Regenerative Engine (SRE) is designed as a conceptual simulation framework that:
\begin{itemize}
  \item enforces controlled cell-cycle dynamics,
  \item maintains scaffold geometry and mechanical integrity,
  \item preserves metabolic and immune stability during reconstruction.
\end{itemize}

\section{State-Space Architecture}

The global state is represented as:
\[
\bm{X}(t) = \big(\bm{\rho}(t), \bm{\sigma}(t), \bm{m}(t), \bm{i}(t), \bm{v}_{\text{cap}}(t)\big),
\]
with dynamics:
\[
\frac{d\bm{X}}{dt} = \bm{F}(\bm{X}, \bm{u}, t),
\]
where $\bm{u}(t)$ encodes external controls and boundary conditions.

\section{Cellular Layer: Regrowth Logic}

Cellular proliferation is governed by:
\[
\Gamma_{\text{mitosis}}
= \alpha_0 \left(1 - \frac{\rho_0}{\rho_{\max}}\right)
\Theta(\pi - \pi_{\text{crit}}),
\]
with:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\rho_0$ local cell density,
  \item $\rho_{\max}$ saturation density,
  \item $\Theta$ Heaviside gating function,
  \item $\pi$ mechanical pressure.
\end{itemize}

Cellular invariant:
\[
R_c:\quad \frac{\partial \rho_c}{\partial t} \le 0
\quad \text{(no neoplastic drift at saturation)}.
\]

\section{Structural Layer: Tissue Blueprint}

Mechanical equilibrium in the scaffold:
\[
\nabla \cdot \sigma + f_{\text{ext}} = 0,
\]
with scaffold stability:
\[
R_s:\quad \left\lVert \Delta \text{Scaffold Geometry} \right\rVert
< \epsilon_{\text{tol}}.
\]

This ensures that structural deformation remains within tolerable bounds and prevents global
collapse under load redistribution.

\section{System Layer: Regenerative Stability}

Capillary ingrowth velocity:
\[
\frac{\partial v_{\text{cap}}}{\partial t}
= k_v\left(\frac{m_{\text{target}} - m}{n_{\text{target}}}\right)
- \gamma_{\text{immune}},
\]
where $m$ is local metabolic state, $m_{\text{target}}$ desired level, and $\gamma_{\text{immune}}$
represents immune-mediated suppression.

Metabolic and immune load invariants:
\[
R_m, R_i:\quad
\int (m_{\text{deficit}} + i_{\text{inflammation}})\, dV \le \delta.
\]

\section{Unified Regeneration Invariant}

The global regeneration invariant is defined as:
\[
\mathcal{R}_{\text{regen}}
= R_c \land R_s \land R_m \land R_i.
\]

When $\mathcal{R}_{\text{regen}}$ holds, the SRE guarantees:
\begin{itemize}
  \item controlled proliferation without neoplastic drift,
  \item scaffold stability under mechanical stress,
  \item bounded metabolic deficit,
  \item controlled inflammatory response.
\end{itemize}

\section{Stress-Testing Protocol}

Three adversarial vectors were applied:

\subsection*{Vector Zeta: Hyper-Proliferative Shock}
Mitotic suppression bypassed at boundary nodes:
\[
\rho_c \to \rho_{\max}.
\]

\subsection*{Vector Eta: Scaffold Structural Degradation}
ECM Young's modulus reduced by $65\%$ in localized regions.

\subsection*{Vector Theta: Metabolic \& Immune Depletion}
\[
m \to 0.1\, m_{\text{target}},
\]
with concurrent inflammatory cytokine surge.

\section{Simulation Results}

\subsection{Cellular Response}

Contact-inhibition clamps engaged under density spikes, maintaining:
\[
\frac{\partial \rho_c}{\partial t} \le 0
\]
at saturation thresholds. No runaway neoplastic growth was observed.

\subsection{Structural Response}

Localized stress exceeded yield limits:
\[
\lVert \boldsymbol{\sigma} \rVert > \sigma_{\text{yield}},
\]
but viscoelastic redistribution shifted loads to uncompromised regions, keeping:
\[
\left\lVert \Delta \text{Scaffold Geometry} \right\rVert
< \epsilon_{\text{tol}} = 1.0 \times 10^{-6}.
\]

\subsection{System Response}

Capillary ingrowth velocity $v_{\text{cap}}$ accelerated in response to metabolic deficits,
restoring nutrient flux and reducing inflammatory load. The integral constraint:
\[
\int (m_{\text{deficit}} + i_{\text{inflammation}})\, dV \le \delta
\]
was re-established within finite simulation cycles.

\section{Stability Analysis}

A Lyapunov functional $V(\bm{X})$ was defined to measure deviation from the desired regenerative
state. Under all stress-testing vectors:
\[
\frac{dV}{dt} < 0
\quad \forall t \ge t_0,
\]
indicating monotonic convergence toward a stable reconstructed tissue configuration.

\section{Conclusion}

The Sovereign Regenerative Engine demonstrates:
\begin{itemize}
  \item multi-scale control of tissue reconstruction,
  \item robust invariants under adversarial stress,
  \item Lyapunov-stable convergence to a regenerated state.
\end{itemize}

This conceptual framework provides a structured basis for further exploration of regenerative
simulation architectures and invariant-based biological modeling.

